# Universal Lifecycle Performance Evidence

> The Awtsmoos renews time, dependency, and memory before finite measurement can speak; Awtsmoos.com records measured evidence without turning one machine's number into eternal decree.

## Static dependency graph

A recursive static-import measurement on the current repository compared direct factory modules:

| Entry | Reachable modules |
| --- | ---: |
| full `createAwtsmoos.js` | 1,538 |
| `createAwtsmoosLifecycle.js` | 133 |

The lifecycle entry avoided 1,406 statically reachable modules, a 91.4% reduction. Of those avoided files, 324 matched heavy Portal/world/Nature/Reality/renderer authority areas in that measurement.

Exact module counts are point-in-time repository evidence, not a semver contract. The stable regression contract is that the lifecycle static graph does not reach heavy expert-world/domain-renderer roots.

## Real browser proof before the latest UX wave

A cache-busted Chrome run of `/apps/universal-procedural/` loaded:

- 157 total resource entries;
- 144 JavaScript resources;
- exactly 133 JavaScript resources from procedural-core;
- 11 JavaScript resources from the demo application.

The 133 procedural-core browser modules matched the recursive lifecycle static graph, proving the page used the intended lightweight entry rather than an accidental broad barrel.

The same development-machine run reached its application-ready marker in 3,693.8 ms. This is local diagnostic context only; no absolute startup SLA is claimed. A new browser run is required after the current accessibility/observability wave before final release evidence is recorded.

## Deterministic cache microbenchmark

On Node v24.17.0 / Darwin x64, three bounded rounds each ran 2,000 plans and 500 identical deterministic compiles. In every round:

- compiler executor invocations: 1;
- cache hits: 499;
- cache misses: 1;
- cache entries: 1.

Observed plan throughput ranged roughly 925–1,783 plans/second and warm compile-call throughput roughly 365–1,338 calls/second under concurrent machine load. Those timings are machine-local diagnostics, not cross-machine guarantees.

## Stable cache contracts

- semantic, request, compiler-chain, constraint-plan, and constraint-resolution identity changes invalidate reuse;
- compiler id/version/support state participate in cache identity;
- environment-dependent compiler or solver execution bypasses deterministic reuse;
- failed compilation must never populate cache;
- repeated deterministic work may reuse immutable compile results;
- planning and explanation remain executor-free.

## Responsive/browser performance principle

Prefer structural assertions over timing thresholds: exact app-ready state, no page-level overflow at narrow widths, same-origin static resources, no severe console/runtime diagnostics, and the expected lifecycle/core module graph. Absolute timing is recorded for diagnosis only.
